"<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"/><title>Mixed Dal Tadka (Pantry Version)</title><script>(function(){
try{
var t=localStorage.getItem('theme');
if(t==='dark'||t==='light')document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme',t);
}catch(e){}
})();</script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/styles/theme.css"/></head><body><div class="site-bar"><a class="site-title" href="/">Recipes</a><button type="button" id="theme-toggle" class="theme-toggle" aria-pressed="false"><span aria-hidden="true">🌗</span><span class="visually-hidden">Toggle dark mode</span></button></div><main class="page"><header><button type="button" class="reset-page-button" data-reset-page="true" aria-label="Reset page to defaults">Reset</button><img class="flag-badge" src="/flags/in.svg" alt width="48" height="48"/><h1>Mixed Dal Tadka (Pantry Version)</h1><p>Serves 4 servings</p><ul class="tags"><li><a href="/tags/indian/"><img class="flag-icon" src="/flags/in.svg" alt width="16" height="16"/>Indian</a></li><li><a href="/tags/vegetarian/">Vegetarian</a></li><li><a href="/tags/vegan/">Vegan</a></li><li><a href="/tags/lentils/">Lentils</a></li></ul></header><section aria-label="Servings" class="servings-widget" data-step="1"><button type="button" aria-label="Decrease servings">−</button><span class="servings-value">4 servings</span><button type="button" aria-label="Increase servings">+</button></section><section aria-label="Units" class="unit-toggle" role="group"><button type="button" data-unit-system="as-written" aria-pressed="true" data-active="true">as-written</button><button type="button" data-unit-system="metric" aria-pressed="false">metric</button><button type="button" data-unit-system="imperial" aria-pressed="false">imperial</button></section><section aria-label="Substitutions" class="substitutions"><h2>Substitutions</h2><button type="button" class="substitution-badge" aria-pressed="false" data-substitution="Make this vegan">Make this vegan</button></section><section aria-label="Ingredients"><h2>Ingredients</h2><div id="ingredients-list"><ul class="ingredient-list"><li>½ cup Chana dal</li><li>½ cup Mung dal (split, yellow)</li><li>3 cup Water (for cooking dal)</li><li>½ tsp Turmeric powder</li><li>1 tsp Salt</li><li>1 Bay leaf</li><li>2 tbsp Ghee</li><li>1 tsp Cumin seeds</li><li>8 Curry leaves</li><li>4 Garlic cloves (minced)</li><li>1 medium Onion (finely chopped)</li><li>1 Tomato (chopped, plum or regular)</li><li>1 tsp Coriander powder</li><li>½ tsp Red chili powder</li><li>0.3 tsp Garam masala</li><li>Pinch of cumin powder (optional)</li><li>Dry chilli (optional)</li></ul></div></section><section aria-label="Steps"><h2>Steps</h2><nav aria-label="Steps view switcher" class="steps-view-switcher"><button type="button" data-view-target="grouped" aria-pressed="true">Grouped</button><button type="button" data-view-target="timeline" aria-pressed="false">Timeline</button><button type="button" data-view-target="mise" aria-pressed="false">Mise en place</button><button type="button" data-view-target="kitchen" aria-pressed="false">Kitchen mode</button></nav><section id="steps-grouped" data-view="grouped"><div class="steps-grouped"><section class="step-group" data-accent="accent-1"><h3>Lentils</h3><ol><li>Rinse the chana dal (½ cup) and mung dal (½ cup) together under cold water until it runs mostly clear.</li><li>Add the rinsed dal to a pot with the water (3 cup), turmeric powder (½ tsp), bay leaf, and half of the salt (½ tsp). Bring to a boil, then reduce heat and simmer, partially covered, until soft and starting to break down.</li><li class="idle">Simmering, partially covered — no action needed.</li><li>Remove the bay leaf. Whisk or lightly mash the dal to your preferred consistency (some like it chunky, some smooth). Set aside.</li></ol></section><section class="step-group" data-accent="accent-2"><h3>Tadka</h3><ol><li>In a small pan, heat the Ghee (2 tbsp) over medium heat. Add the cumin seeds (1 tsp) and let them sizzle for a few seconds, then add the curry leaves (8) and let them crackle.</li><li>Add the garlic (4) and onion (1 medium). Sauté until the onion turns golden.</li><li>Add the tomato (1), coriander powder (1 tsp), and red chili powder (½ tsp). Cook until the tomatoes soften and the oil starts to separate.</li></ol></section><section class="step-group" data-accent="accent-3"><h3>Combine</h3><ol><li>Pour the tempering into the cooked dal. Add the remaining salt (½ tsp) to taste, and stir well. Simmer everything together for a couple minutes to let the flavors meld.</li><li>Turn off the heat and stir in the garam masala (0.3 tsp). Serve hot with rice or roti.</li></ol></section></div></section><section id="steps-timeline" data-view="timeline" hidden><div class="steps-timeline"><ol><li data-accent="accent-1"><span class="timeline-marker">T−60m</span><span class="timeline-group">Lentils</span><span class="timeline-text">Rinse the chana dal (½ cup) and mung dal (½ cup) together under cold water until it runs mostly clear.</span></li><li data-accent="accent-1"><span class="timeline-marker">T−55m</span><span class="timeline-group">Lentils</span><span class="timeline-text">Add the rinsed dal to a pot with the water (3 cup), turmeric powder (½ tsp), bay leaf, and half of the salt (½ tsp). Bring to a boil, then reduce heat and simmer, partially covered, until soft and starting to break down.</span></li><li class="idle" data-accent="accent-1"><span class="timeline-marker">T−20m</span><span class="timeline-group">Lentils</span><span class="timeline-text">Simmering, partially covered — no action needed.</span></li><li data-accent="accent-1"><span class="timeline-marker">T−15m</span><span class="timeline-group">Lentils</span><span class="timeline-text">Remove the bay leaf. Whisk or lightly mash the dal to your preferred consistency (some like it chunky, some smooth). Set aside.</span></li><li data-accent="accent-2"><span class="timeline-marker">T−15m</span><span class="timeline-group">Tadka</span><span class="timeline-text">In a small pan, heat the Ghee (2 tbsp) over medium heat. Add the cumin seeds (1 tsp) and let them sizzle for a few seconds, then add the curry leaves (8) and let them crackle.</span></li><li data-accent="accent-2"><span class="timeline-marker">T−10m</span><span class="timeline-group">Tadka</span><span class="timeline-text">Add the garlic (4) and onion (1 medium). Sauté until the onion turns golden.</span></li><li data-accent="accent-2"><span class="timeline-marker">T−5m</span><span class="timeline-group">Tadka</span><span class="timeline-text">Add the tomato (1), coriander powder (1 tsp), and red chili powder (½ tsp). Cook until the tomatoes soften and the oil starts to separate.</span></li><li data-accent="accent-3"><span class="timeline-marker">T−2m</span><span class="timeline-group">Combine</span><span class="timeline-text">Pour the tempering into the cooked dal. Add the remaining salt (½ tsp) to taste, and stir well. Simmer everything together for a couple minutes to let the flavors meld.</span></li><li data-accent="accent-3"><span class="timeline-marker">T−0m</span><span class="timeline-group">Combine</span><span class="timeline-text">Turn off the heat and stir in the garam masala (0.3 tsp). Serve hot with rice or roti.</span></li></ol></div></section><section id="steps-mise" data-view="mise" hidden><div class="steps-mise"><section class="mise-section" aria-label="Prep checklist"><h4>Prep</h4><ul class="mise-ingredients"><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-key="ingredient-0"/>½ cup Chana dal</label></li><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-key="ingredient-1"/>½ cup Mung dal (split, yellow)</label></li><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-key="ingredient-2"/>3 cup Water (for cooking dal)</label></li><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-key="ingredient-3"/>½ tsp Turmeric powder</label></li><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-key="ingredient-4"/>1 tsp Salt</label></li><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-key="ingredient-5"/>1 Bay leaf</label></li><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-key="ingredient-6"/>2 tbsp Ghee</label></li><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-key="ingredient-7"/>1 tsp Cumin seeds</label></li><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-key="ingredient-8"/>8 Curry leaves</label></li><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-key="ingredient-9"/>4 Garlic cloves (minced)</label></li><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-key="ingredient-10"/>1 medium Onion (finely chopped)</label></li><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-key="ingredient-11"/>1 Tomato (chopped, plum or regular)</label></li><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-key="ingredient-12"/>1 tsp Coriander powder</label></li><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-key="ingredient-13"/>½ tsp Red chili powder</label></li><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-key="ingredient-14"/>0.3 tsp Garam masala</label></li><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-key="ingredient-15"/>Pinch of cumin powder (optional)</label></li><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-key="ingredient-16"/>Dry chilli (optional)</label></li></ul></section><section class="mise-section" aria-label="Method"><h4>Method</h4><ol class="mise-method"><li data-accent="accent-1">Rinse the chana dal (½ cup) and mung dal (½ cup) together under cold water until it runs mostly clear.</li><li data-accent="accent-1">Add the rinsed dal to a pot with the water (3 cup), turmeric powder (½ tsp), bay leaf, and half of the salt (½ tsp). Bring to a boil, then reduce heat and simmer, partially covered, until soft and starting to break down.</li><li class="idle" data-accent="accent-1">Simmering, partially covered — no action needed.</li><li data-accent="accent-1">Remove the bay leaf. Whisk or lightly mash the dal to your preferred consistency (some like it chunky, some smooth). Set aside.</li><li data-accent="accent-2">In a small pan, heat the Ghee (2 tbsp) over medium heat. Add the cumin seeds (1 tsp) and let them sizzle for a few seconds, then add the curry leaves (8) and let them crackle.</li><li data-accent="accent-2">Add the garlic (4) and onion (1 medium). Sauté until the onion turns golden.</li><li data-accent="accent-2">Add the tomato (1), coriander powder (1 tsp), and red chili powder (½ tsp). Cook until the tomatoes soften and the oil starts to separate.</li><li data-accent="accent-3">Pour the tempering into the cooked dal. Add the remaining salt (½ tsp) to taste, and stir well. Simmer everything together for a couple minutes to let the flavors meld.</li><li data-accent="accent-3">Turn off the heat and stir in the garam masala (0.3 tsp). Serve hot with rice or roti.</li></ol></section></div></section><section id="steps-kitchen" data-view="kitchen" hidden><style>
